  "slug": "fitness-dynamics-cb09",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a Genetics paper extend mutation-selection theory to epistasis across environments, mutation rate evolution, and sex allocation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how mutation-selection balance shapes fitness and its distribution across genotypes, environments, and life-history traits. Spectral theory for genetic load shows that load depends on how fitnesses align with mutation-matrix eigenvectors, extending Haldane-Muller results. A global epistasis model fitted to yeast data predicts how non-home fitness changes during adaptation across backgrounds and environments. A multi-locus model shows mutation rates evolving through indirect selection out of equilibrium, and being selected against under homogenising selection. A separate sex allocation model reports continuous stationary distributions of sexual phenotypes under the same balance. Together these works move the focus from equilibrium fitness values toward the dynamics and stationary distributions that mutation and selection generate.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
